## RAG IMPLEMENTATIONS

A production-grade recipe and nutrition application requires a Hybrid Architecture combining Vector RAG, deterministic nutrition APIs (like USDA FoodData Central), and real-time web ingestion. Spoonacular serves as the baseline data schema, while AI orchestrates dynamic retrieval, recipe expansion, and tier-list generation.RAG alone on Spoonacular’s static data will fail to deliver up-to-date trends or mathematically accurate nutrition. The step-by-step system architecture below addresses these limitations.1. Data Expansion Pipeline (Up-to-Date Recipes)Spoonacular Base Layer: Ingest Spoonacular’s core recipe, ingredient, and instruction data into a primary relational database (PostgreSQL) or document store.Dynamic Web Ingestor: Set up a background crawler or search tool (using Tavily API or custom scrapers) targeting reputable culinary sites, food blogs, and trends.Vector Indexing: Extract raw recipe text, convert it into dense vector embeddings using models like text-embedding-3-small or bge-large-en, and store them in a Vector DB (Pinecone, Qdrant, or pgvector). This lets the system fetch newly published recipes beyond Spoonacular's static database.2. Deterministic Nutrition Engine (Deep & Accurate Values)Avoid LLM Math: Large Language Models frequently hallucinate nutritional numbers. The LLM must not guess calories or vitamins.USDA Integration: Map Spoonacular ingredient IDs to the USDA FoodData Central API (Foundation Foods database). This unlocks comprehensive micronutrient data, including complete amino acid profiles, choline, zinc, vitamin K2, and omega ratios that Spoonacular omits.Calculation Layer: Create a backend service that takes ingredient weights from any recipe (Spoonacular or web-scraped), queries the USDA database, and calculates precise aggregate nutrition deterministic values before passing them to the LLM.3. Agentic RAG & Router SystemWhen a user submits a query, an agentic router (built with LangGraph or LlamaIndex) routes the request through a multi-step process:Intent Routing:Factual/Existing Recipe Search: Route directly to Spoonacular / Postgres database.Semantic / Goal-Based Queries ("Show me low-FODMAP high-protein dinners under 30 minutes"): Query the Vector DB.New/Trending Queries ("How to make the 2026 viral pistachio crunch dessert"): Trigger the Live Web Search Tool to fetch and parse a new recipe in real-time.Context Assembly: Combine the retrieved recipe text, the exact calculated USDA nutritional breakdown, and user profile data (allergies, macro goals).LLM Synthesis: Pass the assembled context to an LLM (e.g., Claude 3.5 Sonnet, GPT-4o) to format the response, explain culinary techniques, or suggest ingredient substitutions.4. AI Tier-List & Scoring EngineTransform raw nutrition metrics into qualitative, actionable tier lists using programmatic scoring combined with LLM rationale:Algorithm-Driven Ranking: Calculate objective mathematical metrics for each recipe, such as:Protein Efficiency Ratio: $\text{Grams of Protein} / \text{Total Calories}$Nutrient Density Score: Sum of micronutrients relative to daily recommended intake (DRI).Satiety Index: Ratio of fiber, protein, and volume vs. simple sugars and fats.LLM Tier Generation: Feed these calculated scores into the LLM to categorize recipes into S-Tier, A-Tier, B-Tier, etc., based on specific goals (e.g., Muscle Growth, Longevity/Anti-Inflammatory, Budget Keto). The LLM generates the justification for why a recipe belongs in its assigned tier.Recommended Tech StackDatabase Layer: PostgreSQL + pgvector (stores structured recipes + vector embeddings in one system).Nutrition APIs: Spoonacular API (base structure) + USDA FoodData Central API (deep micronutrients).RAG & Agent Framework: LangGraph or LlamaIndex (handles routing, tool calls, and fallback search).Search Integration: Tavily API or Serper API for fetching fresh recipes off the web.Embedding & LLM: OpenAI text-embedding-3-small for vector search; GPT-4o / Claude 3.5 Sonnet for query routing, tier explanations, and final answer synthesis.
